{:edb/db-before-t 2791 :edb/db-after-t 2792 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536104 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:04:54.168Z" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083031 1145 "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n$P(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n$P(A \\mid D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A \\mid D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D^{′})P(D^{′}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 + 12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083031 1145 "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|D)P(D) + P(A|D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A|D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{11}+\\frac{12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083032 1100 "In box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/94276c7f8de725803347541a3a00e124915f54c89e0908f256f903f32e990c73.png) there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/26eecbf5965af5ad8a5a3ef715bb6e0fa969d36ffafbccaccea2a19616b25eb2.png) marbles, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/a1aa710a9396fec0487bfd728e836f7188caa9a03b1d66d79d88948ba32e32ca.png) of which are green. In box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/762b6cc7cc4d4c8fc3553d61e1779ae5e08597476c0101011b9d3824730e88ed.png) there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/cc635e1b92c71b63fbb85b82a6e5057ccec707d58b8a3a8c64d558004d1a26d1.png) marbles, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/4f94b41c8502c080528c0ca826a6a02aa2e60acba9b8506c41cfb9629e2a2077.png) of which are green. The marbles from the two boxes are mixed, and one marble is randomly chosen from the mixture. What is the probability that the selected marble is green?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083032 1100 "In box $A$, there are $19$ marbles, $7$ of which are green. In box $B$, there are $21$ marbles, $8$ of which are green. The marbles from the two boxes are mixed, and one marble is randomly chosen from the mixture. What is the probability that the selected marble is green?" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083032 1145 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/512ba11ac54abfbb3af0e93bc113799e39e8d81b0925bdf9ddd773b792a9284f.png) be the event that the chosen marble is green, and let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/33afe640f4fdb603b7dfb684ccbdaade53c295b1d26443b458712e4bb55985bf.png) be the event that the chosen marble came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/08bd02340c9c39d2dd0fa163a1f89978e6959c962385733e3f60f82e9e3ccdb8.png) Then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/b43ce7cccfeb3161ca716d624c07927988c87c8b12ebe97974d1901544b7e7ad.png) is the event that the chosen marble came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/756cc5b7560fd9f0a8deecfe5d1f649978b121e1490065242f2a8d0d9a88e122.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/2e831059f27fc478d0e9074c858d317f3a34f40219c0b0b3d98f6f3d67edb74e.png)\n\nTo calculate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/4fb2133dd2933224a6f0d891be21ecbe068a922eaf1f5c17cbf5f370a7ae675b.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/de28878467ff508d37108258a8d0f7985f8898da6e238e98aad8f53f7b2abd49.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/79ed9e30b8d0ce49876979c9ad02d4e6a32c2b3db73cf7c7ebf605c35e95460f.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/2047d3b4bda0c588c7c26a6dd8e9b43b64b5e126cdc155282ee3d296a15554ec.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/16f0eedeee65a71dd9d8fa83535d5f65bff44f1c424663fb2b393046c131e696.png)\n\nSimilarly, to calculate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/9b1bb30112995dd5d0f3e6cd147c26736878279484f82d4d73a086b8efdaec9a.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/27b99f04c6f0f2b24f2d107248a2315f6b2d2574f849bf47379d8f2fdd27d05e.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/b758dc5e36b9ce2daba82e94d58b5232cecff327cf85f3a2571675c5845ba90d.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/75c92641ef9ece42d3dab493a8e8fd78d276cd4f40e423f2b442d23464fb47c7.png) So,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/6ddd96143aa9754eb6b266d82b854a41fcd3e17d9d9c5aeec7d44a669e1994b3.png)\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071098/assets/a1fb9fe4e3ce980bd3385ece84df7522afee0f98b5e57cf82e8c7e0a19a4119c.png)" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083032 1145 "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen marble is green, and let $A$ be the event that the chosen marble was in box $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the chosen marble was in box $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G) = P(G\\cap A) + P(G\\cap A')\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(G\\cap A)$, we take the number of green marbles in box $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes\n\n$$\n(19+21=40)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(G\\cap A')$, we take the number of green marbles in box $B$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes $(40)$. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A') = \\dfrac{8}{40}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(G) &= P(G∩A) + P(G∩A^{′}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{40} + \\frac{8}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083033 1100 "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B$. Of the components produced by machine $A$, $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083033 1100 "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083033 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n$P(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30$.\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n$P(D \\mid A) = 0.08$.\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n$P(D) = 0.11$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D \\mid A')$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D\\begin{vmatrix}A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A^{′})P(A^{′}) \\\\ 0.11|= (0.08)(0.70) + P(D \\mid A^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110|= 0.056 + 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′})|= 0.054 \\\\ P(D\\end{vmatrix}A^{′})|= 0.18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18$." 13194139536104 false] [17592186083033 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D|A)P(A) + P(D|A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A) = 0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = 0.11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|A'),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.11 & =(0.08)(0.70)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110 & =0.056+0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.054 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18.$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383816 13194139536104 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383823 13194139536104 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383830 13194139536104 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383767 13194139536104 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383774 13194139536104 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383795 13194139536104 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383802 13194139536104 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383809 13194139536104 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383781 13194139536104 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383788 13194139536104 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383837 13194139536104 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383844 13194139536104 true] [17592186083866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536104 true] [17592186083866 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nIn a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083866 1100 "In a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083866 1145 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen student plays soccer, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the boys play soccer and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the girls play soccer, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|B)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(S|{B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536104 true] [17592186083872 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B$. She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A$, and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B$. Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?" 13194139536104 false] [17592186083872 1100 "Sophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A,$ and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B.$ Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083872 1145 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $65%$ in account $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.15,\\,P(S|{A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.15)(0.35)+(0.25)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.215.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186083873 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B$. Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$, and $65%$ are in Group $B$. If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265$, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536104 false] [17592186083873 1100 "A math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A,$ and $65%$ are in Group $B.$ If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383768 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1089 "q-122306" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1100 "In a class, $20$ students are boys, and $24$ are girls. $5%$ of the boys are engaged in a sports section, and $25%$ of the girls are engaged in a sports section. What is the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1113 #uuid "6943a89b-51ba-5049-a4c3-036341b2c35b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383767 1145 "Let $S$ be the event that the selected student is engaged in a sports section, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ boys in the class, and $5%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of boys engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\\cdot 20=1.\n$$\n\nThere are $24$  girls in the class, and $25%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of girls  engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}\\cdot 24=6.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number of boys engaged in a sports section $(1)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(20+24=44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{44}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of girls engaged in a sports section $(6)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{6}{44}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{44}+\\frac{6}{44} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{44}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383769 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383770 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383771 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383772 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383773 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383773 13194139536104 true] [17592186383768 1110 #uuid "5f79430e-9462-5ae8-9b51-a3eb667f495c" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383769 1034 "\\frac{3}{44}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383769 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383769 1111 #uuid "7a670870-2343-5277-b441-bde35d4095a8" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383770 1034 "\\frac{13}{22}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383770 1111 #uuid "0feba903-8267-54b7-9df4-15dbc5a8e353" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383771 1034 "\\frac{9}{44}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383771 1111 #uuid "0802490b-c4c2-55b2-ab92-28c76b11d624" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383772 1034 "\\frac{5}{44}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383772 1111 #uuid "e918507c-cf03-5757-82fb-222ad862d1b4" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383773 1034 "\\frac{7}{44}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383773 1111 #uuid "fc461954-364c-5ac9-9512-f69d1908ffa5" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383775 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1089 "q-122397" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1100 "Among the people arrested in a certain city in the mid-1990s, $18%$ were males under $18$ years old and $54%$ were males of $18$ years old and over. What is the probability that a person selected at random from all those arrested is a male?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1113 #uuid "76e576c7-40ca-500f-b543-78612835b8f2" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383774 1145 "Let $M$ be the event that the chosen person is a male, and let $U$ be the event that the chosen person is under $18.$ Then, ${U}^{′}$ is the event that the person is $18$ or over.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is a male, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{′})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap U)=0.18,\\,P(M\\cap {U}^{′})=0.54.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.18+0.54 \\\\  & =0.72.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383776 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383777 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383778 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383779 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383780 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383778 13194139536104 true] [17592186383775 1110 #uuid "29a7bf08-7f81-528b-a7c5-d4b0e9790349" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383776 1034 "0.62" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383776 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383776 1111 #uuid "6cb3b473-683e-52a1-aa46-beb6d66c6746" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383777 1034 "0.18" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383777 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383777 1111 #uuid "ecedb263-cddb-5a5f-b6b4-09b85c85846f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383778 1034 "0.72" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383778 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383778 1111 #uuid "9c481742-707b-5237-884e-167338ca5202" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383779 1034 "0.54" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383779 1111 #uuid "fc9151d2-dbfd-506f-8060-8af36f5c0f9a" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383780 1034 "0.12" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383780 1111 #uuid "ab597d41-4a4e-56cf-ab99-5bf49aec01bd" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383782 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1089 "q-122403" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1100 "Two machines ${M}_{1}$ and ${M}_{2}$ in a particular factory produce $75%$ and $25%,$ respectively, of the total daily output of electronic tubes. It is known that $4%$ of the tubes produced by ${M}_{1}$ are defective and that the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective is $0.0375.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected tube produced by ${M}_{2}$ is defective?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1113 #uuid "aa6ce446-8372-563e-b2d4-b6a175008dba" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383781 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective,  we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected tube from a day's production is defective is $0.0375,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.0375.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.0375 & =(0.04)(0.75)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.25) \\\\ 0.0375 & =0.03+0.25P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.0075 & =0.25P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected tube produced by ${M}_{2}$ is defective is $0.03.$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383783 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383784 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383785 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383786 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383787 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383785 13194139536104 true] [17592186383782 1110 #uuid "7b9ef9dc-7a01-5439-9c40-638f91b5375c" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383783 1034 "0.08" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383783 1111 #uuid "378b1b33-8e5c-5342-85bc-76e07be00442" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383784 1034 "0.15" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383784 1111 #uuid "96902438-963c-5218-adf7-cd6a7a46e7f2" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383785 1034 "0.03" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383785 1111 #uuid "6906ee86-bfa3-5739-9c54-667b86c46fd0" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383786 1034 "0.5" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383786 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383786 1111 #uuid "b2d45c5a-ae2b-50e8-906d-666f6b1282f2" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383787 1034 "0.10" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383787 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383787 1111 #uuid "6536a8d6-bc51-54f9-a0d4-baadba843bcd" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383789 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1089 "q-122412" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1100 "Company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and only $1%$ of their widgets turn out to be defective. Company $B$ supplies the remaining $20%$ of widgets. The probability that a widget from the car shop will be defective is $0.014.$ If a customer randomly purchases a widget supplied by company $B,$ what is the probability that it will be defective?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1113 #uuid "d44f26f0-f74d-5614-a0df-014aa1f39a6b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383788 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that a widget will be defective, and let $A$ be the event that the widget is supplied by company $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the widget is supplied by company $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly purchased widget will be defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since company $A$ supplies $80%$ of widgets for a car shop and company $B$ the remaining $20%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.80,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of the widget supplied by company $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a widget from the car shop is defective will be $0.014,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.014 & =(0.01)(0.80)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.20) \\\\ 0.014 & =0.008+0.20P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.20P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.006 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected widget supplied by company $B$ will be defective is $0.03.$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383790 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383791 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383792 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383793 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383794 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383793 13194139536104 true] [17592186383789 1110 #uuid "321bd09c-e3d3-5497-9946-e7eda90a95f6" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383790 1034 "0.05" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383790 1111 #uuid "70dd2be3-17ea-5aa0-998e-febe1f3b4c52" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383791 1034 "0.3" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383791 1111 #uuid "4631437a-9b6b-5e0d-9d15-50b03bcfd0d2" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383792 1034 "0.13" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383792 1111 #uuid "94590f98-8712-5a8e-a21d-76ed66d3694c" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383793 1034 "0.03" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383793 1111 #uuid "02e76174-bd01-533b-b73f-5c9e5c5849f7" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383794 1034 "0.23" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383794 1111 #uuid "a41b2f5c-cbff-5d4d-bd01-09c4135a062f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383796 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1089 "q-88830" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1100 "In a class, $17$ students are boys, and $23$ are girls. Of the boys, $2$ belong to the school chess club. Of the girls, $3$ are in the chess club. What is the probability that a randomly selected student in the class is in the chess club?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1113 #uuid "95918580-9eee-5598-9f0d-4111cb92fbec" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383795 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen student is on the chess club, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap B),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are boys $(2)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(17+23=40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap B)=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are girls $(3)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383797 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383798 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383799 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383800 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383801 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383798 13194139536104 true] [17592186383796 1110 #uuid "b2c9694e-34c6-5f3f-8b2d-d21304c9cc80" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383797 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383797 1111 #uuid "2e16ff51-48c5-57e3-89a6-9d9d895369b4" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383798 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383798 1111 #uuid "0b50dd2e-223c-5bee-a71e-8e2f3abad80b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383799 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383799 1111 #uuid "3023f36c-eb25-51ae-a411-c717ad0db959" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383800 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383800 1111 #uuid "6fb5b3a4-a852-5123-91fd-6df2bf43bc16" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383801 1034 "\\frac{3}{8}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383801 1111 #uuid "ecdb937b-4541-5ad6-8a20-01a03b4b1c0c" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383803 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1089 "q-88892" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1100 "Alice has two email accounts, $A$ and $B.$ In account $A,$ she received $24$ emails of which $5$ are spam. In the account $B,$ she received $18$ emails of which $2$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email in either account is spam?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1113 #uuid "4f027c79-6eb0-5320-9fc8-81849b6c264f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383802 1145 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email was received in account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email was received in account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number of emails in account A that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{42}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of emails in account $B$ that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{2}{42}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{42}+\\frac{2}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383804 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383805 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383806 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383807 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383808 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383807 13194139536104 true] [17592186383803 1110 #uuid "4882a8ae-982e-563f-9966-d6be916e01ef" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383804 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383804 1111 #uuid "0f6e5f31-2ef6-5826-b822-30771464fb4b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383805 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383805 1111 #uuid "a7c84331-3f1e-5da2-8c93-026e1f2c4a5f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383806 1034 "\\frac{2}{7}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383806 1111 #uuid "bb10ad24-7a0b-5f05-99d2-925d73c1553b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383807 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383807 1111 #uuid "b265767a-d892-530b-a675-91707afd969b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383808 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383808 1111 #uuid "a3c48099-365c-527a-a489-6b77feda06e1" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383810 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1089 "q-88922" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1100 "A gas station offers two types of gasoline, regular and premium. On a certain day, of the $43$ customers who bought regular gas, $23$ paid with a credit card. On the same day, of the $38$ customers that bought premium gas, $31$ of them paid with a credit card. What is the probability of a randomly selected customer paying with a credit card?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1113 #uuid "d0ecdd68-13a0-5025-b2d2-94155866cbce" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383809 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gas. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap R),$ we take the number of people who bought regular gas and paid with a credit card $(23)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(43+38=81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap R)=\\frac{23}{81}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {R}^{′}),$ we take the number of people who bought premium gas and paid with a credit card $(31)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {R}^{′})=\\frac{31}{81}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{81}+\\frac{31}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383811 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383812 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383813 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383814 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383815 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383811 13194139536104 true] [17592186383810 1110 #uuid "e326adc8-e341-55a0-b4b0-956bab5b1d3e" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383811 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383811 1111 #uuid "824b12f8-20c6-5453-a979-a729eaaee723" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383812 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383812 1111 #uuid "2f577ca1-c818-5e5e-a3e2-271bbaa936d2" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383813 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383813 1111 #uuid "a090565b-1a19-5a44-989c-0755c93fd0ff" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383814 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383814 1111 #uuid "66ac889a-eb70-5b87-9794-de4263c4241f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383815 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383815 1111 #uuid "64c11af1-38ec-5056-85d3-f7f60287e49f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383817 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1089 "q-88977" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1100 "A student answers a multiple-choice test. Each question of the test has four possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer for $60%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that she does not know the answer to, what is the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1113 #uuid "4e8c8220-3427-51b3-8588-a7be73c3dc45" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383816 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knew the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student did not know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $60%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.6,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has four possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\  & =(1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.6+0.1 \\\\  & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383818 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383819 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383820 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383821 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383822 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383818 13194139536104 true] [17592186383817 1110 #uuid "4d84c8f8-d6ad-56c7-881d-b4732341da43" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383818 1034 "0.70" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383818 1111 #uuid "01a29caa-0474-5e8d-a7cf-07a5a46279dc" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383819 1034 "0.68" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383819 1111 #uuid "a5eecca0-4a81-5159-9815-0a0e4c3552cd" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383820 1034 "0.72" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383820 1111 #uuid "03d3e4c7-7e88-5d61-91aa-54699b28a5ce" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383821 1034 "0.65" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383821 1111 #uuid "a2556848-ff90-5916-b237-9f6036542386" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383822 1034 "0.75" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383822 1111 #uuid "5bff7b5d-fde0-5ef4-a262-9b2f058cc694" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383824 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1089 "q-89031" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1100 "A certain disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1113 #uuid "408466b2-d5d7-5a6f-92f2-2e2d6c4b81d8" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383823 1145 "Let $A$ be the event that the test is positive for the selected patient, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then, ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient gives a positive result, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P({D}^{′})=\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease, and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,P(A|{D}^{′})=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{10})(\\frac{1}{10})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{9}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{100}+\\frac{9}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383825 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383826 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383827 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383828 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383829 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383828 13194139536104 true] [17592186383824 1110 #uuid "74879bd6-5325-5128-a562-4a6802ea22ae" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383825 1034 "\\frac{9}{10}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383825 1111 #uuid "ab3c0f67-fddf-55d9-bd45-dd88f4b249b8" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383826 1034 "\\frac{1}{11}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383826 1111 #uuid "28f8e32b-32c6-585a-bd63-ddbc1bea88ed" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383827 1034 "\\frac{1}{100}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383827 1111 #uuid "80e8e961-53e9-5144-b814-84be4d81580b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383828 1034 "\\frac{9}{50}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383828 1111 #uuid "b15ea9f0-6569-556b-bff3-708a2d847938" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383829 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383829 1111 #uuid "c3c9143e-4f71-563d-b094-54ce9bb65b7f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383831 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1089 "q-89041" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1100 "A math teacher split their calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Group $A$ has $43%$ of the total number of students, while Group $B$ has $57%$ of the total number of students. If $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade?" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1113 #uuid "846490bd-6254-5a08-8e31-cc536ddf844b" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383830 1145 "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $43%$ of their students are in Group $A$ and $57%$ are in Group $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.43,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.23,\\,P(G|{A}^{′})=0.19.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.23)(0.43)+(0.19)(0.57) \\\\  & ≈0.207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383832 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383833 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383834 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383835 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383836 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383832 13194139536104 true] [17592186383831 1110 #uuid "fdf28614-efc7-52f0-bd6a-d1efea9c55ae" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383832 1034 "0.207" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383832 1111 #uuid "961152f7-bee5-558f-bce0-6ef9f0c80ef7" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383833 1034 "0.197" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383833 1111 #uuid "be3c9adf-bc12-52f3-a569-7a970a8a76bd" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383834 1034 "0.227" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383834 1111 #uuid "8642fbe2-99a1-5834-97ce-6dcc27e3977a" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383835 1034 "0.213" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383835 1111 #uuid "87ab7379-f694-5997-b5bb-f6d10fe3535e" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383836 1034 "0.312" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383836 1111 #uuid "6d4b2075-eb10-5e2e-a960-abbdce3f9921" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383838 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1089 "q-89042" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1100 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the total components manufactured, $60%$ of them are produced by machine $A,$ and the remaining $40%$ are produced by machine $B.$ Of the components produced by machine $A,$ $10%$ are defective. In total, $12%$ of all components produced are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is selected at random, what is the probability of it being defective? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1113 #uuid "b38f87cd-849d-519c-9592-01abf17dbe8f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383837 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $60%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $40%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.60,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.40.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.10.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of the components produced are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.12 & =(0.10)(0.60)+P(D|{A}^{′})(0.40) \\\\ 0.12 & =0.06+0.40P(D|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.40P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.06 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{′}) & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.15.$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383839 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383840 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383841 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383842 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383843 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383841 13194139536104 true] [17592186383838 1110 #uuid "29a91ee7-75c1-5c81-9b3e-691604156c90" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383839 1034 "0.17" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383839 1111 #uuid "2fa45848-efd9-5d23-a679-2315501d86c7" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383840 1034 "0.25" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383840 1111 #uuid "8b4e8b2e-23f0-51fa-8bbd-c91a4a75ba9d" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383841 1034 "0.15" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383841 1111 #uuid "c26868d0-20af-533e-acc3-1b08c868e97f" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383842 1034 "0.20" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383842 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383842 1111 #uuid "cd38b16a-8193-5ee8-a86e-07cbc5bed587" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383843 1034 "0.12" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383843 1111 #uuid "990519d1-feee-5178-9796-c20ad96631c4" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383845 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1089 "q-89053" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1100 "In a gas station, $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline. If $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline usually pay with a credit card and the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1113 #uuid "9f71c3d4-298d-5849-a475-518d54df8b50" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383844 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person pays with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person buys regular gas. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person buys premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person pays with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.55,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline pay with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.645.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|{R}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.645 & =(0.60)(0.55)+P(C|{R}^{′})(0.45) \\\\ 0.645 & =0.33+0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.315 \\\\ P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card is $0.70.$" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383846 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383847 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383848 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383849 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383850 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1041 "selection" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383848 13194139536104 true] [17592186383845 1110 #uuid "f522b0f2-f7d7-56bf-aee8-db36961299fc" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383846 1034 "0.80" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383846 1111 #uuid "0436c47e-08d5-5b11-a423-5e72c7ed4d42" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383847 1034 "0.60" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536104 true] [17592186383847 1111 #uuid "3c9ac833-3359-5a89-8200-baf67ed28b0d" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383848 1034 "0.70" 13194139536104 true] [17592186383848 11